\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Contact floor positivity and medium adjacency in finite weighted graphs.} (\textbf  {A})~Contact floor $\beta _{\min }$ (log scale) for five representative finite weighted graphs of increasing structural complexity, confirming Lemma~\ref {lem:floor_positive}: the minimum edge weight is strictly positive in every graph, spanning six orders of magnitude from $\beta _{\min }= 10^{-9}$ to $\beta _{\min }= 0.5$. No graph achieves $\beta _{\min }= 0$. (\textbf  {B})~Wheel graph with medium $\mathcal  {M}$ at the centre and nodes $v_0,\ldots  ,v_7$ arranged on the rim, coloured by their edge weight to the medium $w(v_i, \mathcal  {M}) \in \{1,\ldots  ,8\}$ on a sequential scale. The topology enforces the medium's defining property (Definition~\ref {def:medium}): every node is adjacent to $\mathcal  {M}$ with positive weight. (\textbf  {C})~Edge weights $w(v_i, \mathcal  {M})$ for all eight rim nodes shown as horizontal bars. The uniform spacing confirms that the medium is in contact with every node and that every contact has cost $\geq \beta _{\min }> 0$: individuation is never free. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional embedding of the wheel graph: nodes placed at $(r\cos \theta _i, r\sin \theta _i, w_i)$ where $\theta _i = 2\pi i/8$ and $w_i = w(v_i, \mathcal  {M})$, with the medium at the origin. The vertical axis is the edge weight to the medium; the spread in $z$ reflects the distinct individuation depths of the eight nodes, none of which reaches $z = 0$.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mafg-panel-01}{{1}{5}{\textbf {Contact floor positivity and medium adjacency in finite weighted graphs.} (\textbf {A})~Contact floor $\bmin $ (log scale) for five representative finite weighted graphs of increasing structural complexity, confirming Lemma~\ref {lem:floor_positive}: the minimum edge weight is strictly positive in every graph, spanning six orders of magnitude from $\bmin = 10^{-9}$ to $\bmin = 0.5$. No graph achieves $\bmin = 0$. (\textbf {B})~Wheel graph with medium $\med $ at the centre and nodes $v_0,\ldots ,v_7$ arranged on the rim, coloured by their edge weight to the medium $w(v_i, \med ) \in \{1,\ldots ,8\}$ on a sequential scale. The topology enforces the medium's defining property (Definition~\ref {def:medium}): every node is adjacent to $\med $ with positive weight. (\textbf {C})~Edge weights $w(v_i, \med )$ for all eight rim nodes shown as horizontal bars. The uniform spacing confirms that the medium is in contact with every node and that every contact has cost $\geq \bmin > 0$: individuation is never free. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional embedding of the wheel graph: nodes placed at $(r\cos \theta _i, r\sin \theta _i, w_i)$ where $\theta _i = 2\pi i/8$ and $w_i = w(v_i, \med )$, with the medium at the origin. The vertical axis is the edge weight to the medium; the spread in $z$ reflects the distinct individuation depths of the eight nodes, none of which reaches $z = 0$}{figure.1}{}}
